1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,969
Chunking and retrieval quality get most of the attention in a RAG project, and

2
00:00:04,969 --> 00:00:08,285
they're not what actually stalls enterprise deployments.

3
00:00:08,285 --> 00:00:13,023
What stalls them is realising, usually late, that a document carries information

4
00:00:13,023 --> 00:00:17,582
retrieval never checks: who's allowed to see it, and whether it's still true.

5
00:00:17,582 --> 00:00:19,536
Both problems share a root cause.

6
00:00:19,536 --> 00:00:21,905
An embedding represents what text means.

7
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:25,458
It has no opinion on access, and no opinion on the calendar.

8
00:00:25,458 --> 00:00:26,998
That's worth sitting with.

9
00:00:26,998 --> 00:00:30,314
Why are both failures so easy to miss in your own pilot?

10
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:35,288
A pilot usually runs with one tester, who has full access to everything, on a corpus

11
00:00:35,288 --> 00:00:36,887
that was indexed yesterday.

12
00:00:36,887 --> 00:00:40,736
Neither failure mode has anywhere to hide under those conditions.

13
00:00:40,736 --> 00:00:45,651
Both show up the moment the pilot becomes a real rollout: many users with different

14
00:00:45,651 --> 00:00:49,027
access, a corpus that's now months old and still growing.

15
00:00:49,577 --> 00:00:54,621
Four assumptions, and every one of them holds fine in a demo and fails quietly in

16
00:00:54,621 --> 00:00:59,853
production, because a demo runs with one user and a snapshot nobody's had time to go

17
00:00:59,853 --> 00:01:00,227
stale.

18
00:01:00,227 --> 00:01:05,396
The permission rows matter because your organisation's documents were never written

19
00:01:05,396 --> 00:01:07,078
with one flat access level.

20
00:01:07,078 --> 00:01:08,261
HR policy, general.

21
00:01:08,261 --> 00:01:10,192
Compensation bands, restricted.

22
00:01:10,192 --> 00:01:12,621
A draft strategy memo, leadership only.

23
00:01:12,621 --> 00:01:17,852
Index all of it into one pool and ask a single retrieval system to search across it,

24
00:01:17,852 --> 00:01:22,897
and the system has no idea it's supposed to treat those three differently, unless

25
00:01:22,897 --> 00:01:25,575
someone explicitly built that filtering in.

26
00:01:25,575 --> 00:01:30,620
The freshness rows are the quieter risk, precisely because a stale answer doesn't

27
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:31,617
announce itself.

28
00:01:31,617 --> 00:01:36,599
A permission leak at least produces a document someone can point to and say this

29
00:01:36,599 --> 00:01:38,094
shouldn't have shown up.

30
00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:42,827
An answer built on a policy that changed three months ago just sounds right.

31
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:47,872
It reads exactly like a current answer would, because the mechanism generating it

32
00:01:47,872 --> 00:01:51,858
doesn't know the difference between old truth and current truth.

33
00:01:52,408 --> 00:01:57,663
The difference is exactly one step, inserted at exactly one point: before results go

34
00:01:57,663 --> 00:01:59,415
back to the user, not after.

35
00:01:59,415 --> 00:02:04,045
Notice the second column doesn't require re-architecting retrieval itself.

36
00:02:04,045 --> 00:02:09,300
It requires the retrieval step to ask a second question alongside "what's relevant":

37
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:14,430
what is this specific person actually allowed to see, checked against their access

38
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:18,747
right now, not their access when the document was indexed months ago.

39
00:02:18,747 --> 00:02:23,752
This is also exactly the kind of decision from Section three's PRD template that

40
00:02:23,752 --> 00:02:29,007
belongs in writing before you launch, not discovered during a security review after.

41
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:33,950
Naming who owns the access model, and how quickly a revoked permission actually

42
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:39,205
takes effect, is a specification question with a real owner, the same way the golden

43
00:02:39,205 --> 00:02:40,957
dataset and the rubric were.

44
00:02:41,507 --> 00:02:46,525
Say the honest caveat, because freshness has a limit that no amount of engineering

45
00:02:46,525 --> 00:02:48,606
effort around RAG actually solves.

46
00:02:48,606 --> 00:02:53,686
A policy that updates twice a year fits RAG comfortably: re-index after each change

47
00:02:53,686 --> 00:02:54,849
and you're current.

48
00:02:54,849 --> 00:02:59,929
Live inventory, today's exchange rate, a support ticket's status right now, changes

49
00:02:59,929 --> 00:03:03,479
faster than any reasonable re-indexing schedule can track.

50
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:06,295
That content was never meant to be a document.

51
00:03:06,295 --> 00:03:11,252
It's a live answer from a live system, and the right fix is a direct call to that

52
00:03:11,252 --> 00:03:16,271
system at the moment of the question, not a retrieval index trying to keep up with

53
00:03:16,271 --> 00:03:18,536
something that changes by the minute.

54
00:03:18,536 --> 00:03:23,371
Section six picks this up properly: giving a model a tool to call, instead of a

55
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:24,534
document to search.

56
00:03:24,534 --> 00:03:29,063
This is worth checking against the decision tree from Section two as well.

57
00:03:29,063 --> 00:03:32,062
"Needs facts that change" pointed you toward RAG.

58
00:03:32,062 --> 00:03:36,958
It's worth adding the missing half of that sentence now: needs facts that change

59
00:03:36,958 --> 00:03:39,590
slower than you can realistically re-index.

60
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:43,201
Past that speed, the honest answer isn't a better pipeline.

61
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:45,772
It's a different box on the tree entirely.

62
00:03:46,322 --> 00:03:51,335
That's the definition worth holding onto, and notice the word doing the real work is

63
00:03:51,335 --> 00:03:51,634
live.

64
00:03:51,634 --> 00:03:53,424
Checked now, not checked once.

65
00:03:53,424 --> 00:03:55,871
The exercise is uncomfortable on purpose.

66
00:03:55,871 --> 00:03:58,438
Picture someone leaving your company today.

67
00:03:58,438 --> 00:04:03,451
Ask, specifically, how long their old access lingers in your retrieval system before

68
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:04,585
it's actually gone.

69
00:04:04,585 --> 00:04:09,420
If nobody in the room knows the answer, that's the honest finding, and it's worth

70
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:11,986
surfacing before an auditor finds it first.

71
00:04:12,536 --> 00:04:14,333
One thing before you move on.

72
00:04:14,333 --> 00:04:18,546
Find the real answer to the access question, not the reassuring one.

73
00:04:18,546 --> 00:04:23,255
Minutes, hours, or the next scheduled re-index are three very different risk

74
00:04:23,255 --> 00:04:25,237
profiles wearing the same shrug.

75
00:04:25,237 --> 00:04:30,318
Then name one piece of content that's genuinely too fast-moving for retrieval, the

76
00:04:30,318 --> 00:04:33,044
way live inventory or a real-time status is.

77
00:04:33,044 --> 00:04:37,938
Flagging it now saves you from building a RAG feature for something that always

78
00:04:37,938 --> 00:04:39,611
needed a tool call instead.

79
00:04:39,611 --> 00:04:44,258
Next lecture puts everything from this section into the RAG design doc: the

80
00:04:44,258 --> 00:04:49,276
pipeline, the chunking strategy, the metrics, and both of today's hard realities,

81
00:04:49,276 --> 00:04:50,887
walked through end to end.
